<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Overlay knowledgebase"
  description="The high-level shape of the ~170 ARM9 overlays — what they are, how they load, and why patching them is delicate. The full per-overlay catalog is live; see Overlays in the nav."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'How the Game Works', href: `${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/` },
    { label: 'Overlay knowledgebase' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    This page is the "what's an overlay" explainer. The
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/how-the-game-works/overlays/`}>overlay system</a>
    page covers the technical specifics (RAM slots, controller/body
    pattern, persistent engines). The full per-overlay catalog — one row per
    overlay, with confirmed role, notable functions, container references,
    and embedded JP strings — lives at
    <a href={`${base}/overlays/`}>the Overlays section</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What is an overlay?</h2>
  <p>
    The DS has very little RAM (4 MB total) and Tongari Boushi is a fairly
    large game. To fit, the engine breaks its code into <strong>overlays</strong>:
    chunks of compiled ARM9 code that are loaded from the cartridge into a
    shared RAM region only when they're needed. When the player moves between
    game modes — exiting a shop, opening the mail box, entering the cooking
    minigame — the engine evicts the current overlay and loads the next one
    into the same address.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are <strong>180 overlays</strong> in this game. A handful are
    resident the whole time the game is running (the 2D rendering engine
    <code>ov137</code> is the big one); the rest swap in and out.
  </p>

  <h2>How an overlay loads</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>The state machine in the main ARM9 binary decides "we're now in mode X."</li>
    <li>It looks up overlay X in the overlay table at ROM offset <code>0x8DE00</code>.</li>
    <li>That table row says: load file <code>overlay/overlay_NNNN.bin</code>, decompress if needed, and copy it to address <code>ram_addr</code>.</li>
    <li>If the destination RAM slot already holds a different overlay, that overlay is overwritten.</li>
    <li>The new overlay's entrypoint runs; it sets up state, registers event handlers, and yields back to the main loop.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Compression — BLZ for ARM9, code-LZ for overlays</h2>
  <p>
    The main ARM9 binary at ROM offset <code>0x4000</code> ships
    BLZ-compressed (Nintendo's backward-running LZ variant used for
    executable code). Most overlay files at <code>overlay/overlay_NNNN.bin</code>
    ship with their <code>compressed_flag</code> set, meaning the engine runs
    a code-LZ decompressor on them at load time. The
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/blz-arm9/`}>BLZ &amp; the plaintext-ARM9 trick</a>
    page covers the format details, the in-place backward decompression, the
    NitroSDK module-params layout, and the "flip <code>CompressedStaticEnd</code>"
    trick the patcher uses to load ARM9 as plaintext.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same trick applies to overlays: flipping the per-overlay
    <code>is_compressed</code> bit in the overlay table to <code>0</code> and
    storing the overlay body uncompressed lets the patcher edit overlay code
    in place without re-running the encoder. v2.31 fixed a long-standing crash
    in the friend menu / sing / hum / clothing codec / bug-register flow that
    came from this compressed-flag bookkeeping; v2.32 ships those fixes
    intact, with overlay strings re-LZ-compressed via
    <code>ndspy.codeCompression</code> for an on-disk shape closer to the
    Konami original.
  </p>

  <h2>NitroSDK validation markers — none present</h2>
  <p>
    Retail DS binaries from some publishers carry the NitroSDK code footer
    markers (<code>0xDEC00621</code> "NitroCodeBE", <code>0x2106AED5</code>
    "NitroFooter") used to validate plaintext ARM9 modules at load time.
    Konami stripped or never emitted them on this ROM —
    <strong>all 180 overlays scan clean for both markers</strong>, and the
    main ARM9 plaintext doesn't carry them either. This is normal for retail
    DS titles where overlays ship BLZ-compressed; the markers wouldn't
    survive decompression intact anyway. The patcher does not need to
    re-emit them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why patching overlays is delicate</h2>
  <p>
    Most of our v2.x release work happens inside container files
    (<code>data/*</code>) — the dialog strings, item tables, magic glyphs.
    Those are safe to patch because the engine reads them at runtime and
    doesn't care about their internal layout as long as the file format is
    valid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Overlays are different. An overlay is <em>compiled code</em>. It has
    hard-coded addresses, fixed-stride data tables, and pointers into
    <code>.rodata</code> that were resolved at link time. If you change a
    string or a constant inside an overlay you have to either:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Keep the patch byte-exact in length</strong> — replace 4 bytes
      with exactly 4 bytes, never more — so nothing else in the overlay shifts; or</li>
    <li><strong>Recompute every pointer in the overlay</strong> that pointed
      past your edit, which means re-resolving the relocation table by hand.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Get either of those wrong and the game crashes, usually as soon as the
    overlay is loaded. So overlay edits are surgical: the patch tools track
    each overlay edit as a specific (file, offset, before-bytes, after-bytes)
    tuple and refuse to apply if the original bytes don't match.
  </p>

  <h2>What v2.31 / v2.32 changed</h2>
  <p>
    Version <strong>2.31</strong> and <strong>2.32</strong> both touch the
    same six overlays: <code>ov047</code>, <code>ov059</code>,
    <code>ov065</code>, <code>ov096</code>, <code>ov170</code>, and
    <code>ov171</code>. Each edit is a small, length-preserving string or
    constant change. v2.31 also fixed the engine-side compressed-flag bug
    that was preventing menus driven by these overlays (friend menu, sing/hum,
    clothing codec, bug register) from loading reliably. v2.32 ships those
    fixes intact and adds nothing new to the overlay surface — its content
    work concentrates on the bulletin/letter corpus, song-name lists, and
    NPC name typography (all of which live in container files, not overlay
    code). See the
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/arm9-menu-pool/`}>ARM9 menu pool</a>
    page for the per-slot EN strings shipped in those six overlays.
  </p>
  <p>
    Of the other 174 overlays, none have been edited yet. Most are read-only
    in the patch sense — we know what they do but we don't need to change
    them.
  </p>

